-- Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two, six trucks, trailers and cars (printed 206-215): All-Purpose Trailers, Big Bertha, Cargonaut, Crisis Response Vehicle, Ermine, Pathmaker
-- 6 vehicle rows, with 57 locations and 11 weapon entries.
--
-- One-off data script, run once per environment. NOT a migration - it adds
-- rows, not schema.
--
--   node scripts/d1-apply.mjs --local apps/character-creator/db/~324-northern-gun-2-trucks-a.sql
--
-- Written by scripts/vessel-sql.mjs from 1 worker file(s). Every value
-- is printable ASCII, every row cites its book, and a Main Body location
-- agrees with its vehicle's mdc_main_body.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO vehicles (slug, name, system, vehicle_class, crew, passengers, speed_ground, speed_air, speed_water, dimensions, weight_tons, mdc_main_body, is_mega_damage, ar, cost, cost_note, description, source_book) VALUES
  ('ng-apt-all-purpose-trailer', 'APT All-Purpose Trailer', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'Varies with the trailer type.', 'Varies with type: the Adventurers'' Trailer houses eight; the Command and Control Bunker Trailer holds up to 16.', 'None under its own power; a large, heavy vehicle must tow it. Towing one cuts the tow vehicle''s speed by 20% and costs -10% piloting, both doubled on sand and in snow up to 2 ft (0.6 m). Thick brush, woodland, and deep water, mud or snow are beyond it.', NULL, NULL, 'Height 12 ft (3.7 m), or open with no walls as a simple flatbed; width 12 ft (3.7 m); length 25-30 ft (7.6-9.1 m)', '2-8 tons, by type', 300, 1, NULL, 20000, 'By type: a plain flatbed 20,000 credits; Cargo Trailer 45,000 unrefrigerated or 95,000 refrigerated, with power extra (10,000-20,000 for a generator, 300,000 Solid Oxide, 800,000 nuclear with a 12 year life, rare); Adventurers'' Trailer 250,000 on batteries with solar backup, 540,000 Solid Oxide, 850,000 nuclear; Medical Trailer 2.3 million; Command and Control Bunker Trailer 2.5 million; Repair Station & Garage Trailer 3.5 million, always nuclear. Replacement M.D.C. tires are 1,600 credits each, armored ones 6,000.', 'Northern Gun''s All-Purpose Trailer (APT) line, hitched behind heavy ground machines (Big Bertha, Cargonaut, Mountaineer, Pathmaker, Salamander, Wolftrack), or other trucks, military vehicles and hovertrains. The page states that every type shares one set of basic statistics whatever its fittings, and gives each type a paragraph and a price rather than a stat block, so this is one row. Oversized wheels suit mixed ground. Tires: losing one or two costs no speed but -5% piloting; losing all three on one side cuts speed 40%, unbalances the load and costs -20%, and dragging on risks (01-40% per 15 minutes) the trailer tearing loose and bursting, spilling 1D4x10% of its load while trailer and contents take 1D4x10 M.D. Cargo: an empty cargo trailer carries up to 40 tons. Power: electric, Solid Oxide or nuclear. Weapons: none standard; as many as six may be added at extra cost, which few buyers do. Sensors: most types have none. Types: Adventurers'' Trailer, a mobile home for eight with two doors, a kitchen, folding beds that become seating, a latrine, a tiny shower and water and waste tanks, stocked for a month (two on hard rations) and short only of privacy; batteries and a fold-out roof solar array run it (24 hours a charge, four hours of sun to refill) with a backup generator good for 48 hours on a tank of fuel or one E-Clip, and buyers now usually add a computer station, a small armory and an E-Clip charger taking four clips at once (an hour for all four); NG advises its owners to avoid fights, and any roof weapons cost extra. Cargo Trailer, with internal rails locking down 40 tons plus tie-downs, refrigerated (with a small power source) or not. Command and Control Bunker Trailer, on two decks: the lower command centre (the text gives it 400 M.D.C.) seats eight at monitors with the commander''s 52 inch screen in front and encrypted communications reaching 500 miles (800 km); the upper radar and communications tower holds 2-4 specialists (120 M.D.C. in the text, 75 under its Additional M.D.C. line). Fully staffed by nine, it gives the allied forces it directs +1 each on Perception Rolls, to strike with long-range weapons, to dodge, and to save vs Horror Factor and morale attacks; it holds 16, 2-4 of them guards in power armor or special forces. Its Main Body M.D.C. is doubled, and its windows close behind sliding 30 M.D.C. plates. Medical Trailer, a small mobile hospital: an open eight-table surgery, two sealed operating rooms, a lab, eight beds, supply rooms, scanners (x-ray, MRI, CAT, EKG and others) and a first stock of medical nanobot kits; parked, its sides and awnings open to take ten more patients on cots, and on the move it keeps half its beds working; Cyber-Docs may trade half the beds for a cybernetics and bionics workshop. Repair Station & Garage Trailer, usable only when parked: it splits open on fold-out pylons, with a rear ramp robots and power armor walk up, side scaffolds that extend to straddle a larger robot, tool cabinets, a big air compressor, a nuclear generator that can recharge or power other vehicles, a crane reaching 30 ft (9.1 m) and lifting 20 tons, and eight power armor bays (its price includes tools for six). It cancels the penalty for repairs in battlefield conditions, works on body armor, weapons and gear too, and its diagnostic computer shows any NG machine''s damage and parts list in 1D4 melee rounds; other North American makes take three times as long with a 2D4% margin of error, and machines from other continents or dimensions cannot be read.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.206-207'),
  ('ng-hch-2000-big-bertha', 'NG-HCH-2000 Big Bertha Heavy Cargo Hauler', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'Two: a pilot and a co-pilot or communications engineer.', 'Two, with a sleeping area for four.', 'Cab alone with no cargo: 375 mph (600 km) maximum; cargo slows it sharply. Hauling, it usually hovers 2-5 ft (0.6-1.5 m) up, never higher than the containers in tow allow. Under 450 tons: up to 125 mph (200 km). From 450 tons to its 850 ton limit: 60 mph (96 km) maximum.', 'Hover only: the unladen cab reaches 375 mph (600 km) at no more than 1,000 ft (305 m) of altitude.', 'Cab and empty containers skim the surface at most at 187 mph (299.2 km/162.4 knots); with every container sealed airtight it runs underwater at 90 mph (144 km/78 knots) to a depth of 1.5 miles (2.4 km). Hauling under 450 tons: 62.5 mph (100 km) on the surface, 41.7 mph (67 km) underwater; hauling 450-850 tons: 30 mph (48 km) and 20 mph (32 km). Water travel needs the rig in good order: below half its Main Body M.D.C., underwater travel is risky and the safe depth halves.', 'Height 44 ft 5 in (13.5 m); width 52 ft (15.8 m), spreading to 68 ft (20.7 m) for extra-wide containers; length 90 ft 8 in (27.6 m) from cab front to thruster tips', '53.7 tons, plus cargo', 540, 1, NULL, 69000000, '69 million credits new; 40-52 million used or rebuilt, likely with 20% less M.D.C. and 10% less speed. Good availability from Northern Gun, fair from its other sellers.', 'Model NG-HCH-2000 (Class: Heavy Cargo Hauler Hover Tractor Truck). Released in 108 P.A., an early member of NG''s widening family of heavy haulers. Hovertrains run in numbers only in Upper Michigan and on a few trade routes, and their crews shun small drops off the safe trails, so rigs like this carry goods and big machines into remote, dangerous country such as the Pecos Empire, New West, Mexico, Canada and the Magic Zone. It is a hover tractor cab, armoured like a Coalition tank and twice that size, pulling either 1-4 hover boxcars or 2-8 trailers on wheels through a universal modular hitch. Twelve big hover jets under the rig lift it, landing gear props it up when parked, and two 70 ft (21.3 m) side thruster arms, spread hydraulically to fit wide loads, drive it with a jet-engine roar, so the cab is soundproofed. It has no windows: hidden cameras on every side show the driver his surroundings. The cab holds the pilot''s station, two passenger seats, bunks for four, a small washroom, a gun rack and a locker the size of a walk-in closet. One or two containers can be clamped and carried outright, up to 140 tons (-5% piloting); more on wheels, treads or hover systems trail behind, to 850 tons in all: semi-trailers, boxcars, hovertrain containers, APTs, or NG''s armoured SCC-90 Super-Cargo Carrier (188 ft/57.3 m long, 50 ft/15.2 m wide, 68 ft/20.7 m tall, holding 850 tons). Losing six hover jets halves speed. Piloting penalties, all cumulative: -10% at all times, -10% for a quick stop, -20% hauling over 400 tons, -30% to dodge, evade or stunt. Power: nuclear, about 12 years. Weapons: none; valuable loads usually travel with escorts. Two weapons can be mounted on the cab top and sides and on the thrusters (never the container), but owners seldom do it: guns draw fire, many towns bar armed combat vehicles, an armed driver cannot pass as a peaceful civilian, and insurance will not pay for raids. Sensors: standard robot-vehicle basics.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.207-209'),
  ('ng-c100-cargonaut', 'NG-C100 Cargonaut Modular Utility Vehicle', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'Up to 3; four more in the personal, taxi and pickup versions (riding in the bed of the pickup), or 8 in the minibus.', 'Wheels and tracks (the commonest) 80 mph (128 km); tracks only 50 mph (80 km); hover jets 80 mph (128 km). Pulling 20 tons or more costs 20%, and going off-road costs another 20% (except on hover jets). Half speed or worse on grassland, sandy desert and rough ground; woodland and thick brush are likely impassable without a wide, well-worn path, and even then under 10 mph (16 km).', 'The hover version cannot fly; it hovers up to 10 ft (3 m) above the ground.', 'None; the hover version cannot hover on or under water.', 'Height 8-12 ft (2.4-3.7 m); width 8 ft (2.4 m) or more, wider pulling a trailer; length 14-30 ft (4.3-9.1 m)', '2-4 tons', 140, 1, NULL, 90000, '90,000 credits liquid fuel, 180,000 electric, 520,000 Solid Oxide, 975,000 nuclear (10 year life); the hover Cargonaut costs 385,000 more.', 'Model NG-C100 (Class: Modular Utility Vehicle). One of NG''s best-selling vehicles ever, though few people recognise it: NG offers more than forty versions, NG and independent firms sell military add-on kits, and paint, rust and field repairs do the rest. Before hovertrains became NG''s supply line, convoys of Cargonauts carried its goods to its dealers. Versions include private cars, vans, pickups, tow and dump trucks, tractors, bulldozers, forklifts, taxis, minibuses, street sweepers, ATVs, military jeeps and small tankers, on wheels, tracks or hover jets, plus a winter snowmobile; some carry guns and some carry schoolchildren. The book''s picture shows a general cargo truck. Every one is a heavy-duty truck with independent suspension and a locking slide rail, so a module (passenger pod, flatbed, boxcar, fuel tank, APT, forklift, crane, backhoe and so on) swaps in minutes unless the owner has over-customised it. Penalties: none on pavement, short grass, dirt roads and stony desert; -10% piloting on gravel and tall grass, -15% on broken roads, sand, and water, mud or snow up to a foot (0.3 m) deep, except for the hover version; deeper mud and snow, forest and dense growth are likely impassable. It can cross sand. Range: 300 miles (480 km) on gasoline, 2,000 miles (3,200 km) a charge on the upgraded multi-battery electric system, unlimited on Solid Oxide or nuclear; the liquid-fuel engine burns gasoline, diesel, ethanol or strong alcohol mixes. Cargo: any version tows APTs and up to 55 tons; the cab has a glove box and under-seat storage. Weapons: none standard, though the pickup and military jeep versions in particular suit mounted guns.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.210'),
  ('ng-crv05-crisis-response-vehicle', 'NG-CRV05 Crisis Response Vehicle', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver, with room for two passengers in the cab.', 'By module: SWAT seats six officers in power armor or ten in EBA; Arrest holds 1-4 in each of two small cells and 6-8 in the long cell, plus up to four guards; APC carries 6-8 power armor troops, 10-14 in light armor or EBA, or two hovercycles with riders and 2-6 more troops.', '70 mph (112 km) maximum on pavement and dirt roads, half that on gravel and short grass. Not built for off-road use.', NULL, NULL, 'Height 11 ft (3.4 m); width 8 ft (2.4 m); length 24 ft (7.3 m)', '2 tons unloaded; most modules add two tons, the APC four', 260, 1, NULL, 620000, '620,000 credits electric, 800,000 Solid Oxide, 1.2 million nuclear (about 12 years), for the empty truck only. Modules cost extra: Medical 760,000; Police Mobile Command 575,000 without its two robot drones; Police SWAT 679,000; Police Arrest 375,000; APC 890,000 with its first load of missiles.', 'Model NG-CRV05 (Class: Modular Armored Personnel Carrier Crisis Response Vehicle). From a design team led by Matthew Clements, Michael Collins, Bill Sheets and Susan Beasley: a tough armoured truck whose rear section takes interchangeable, self-contained emergency modules, slid in or out by two heavy forklifts or a pair of robots and held by eight big bolts, so a town or mercenary company may own one or two trucks and several modules to swap by the emergency. Rear-compartment windows have 20 M.D.C. and a sliding alloy plate, closed by button or voice, that adds 50 more. With the Main Body gone, the truck dies, its electronics are ruined, and its doors keep 1D4x10 M.D.C. until that too is shot away. Penalties: -5% piloting on dirt roads, stony desert and short grass; -10% on tall grass, gravel, broken roads and rough ground, and in water, mud or snow to 1 ft (0.3 m); -30% deeper, to 2.5 ft (0.76 m); thick brush, woodland, swamp and sandy desert are impassable, ram prow or not. Cargo: about 3 tons inside when empty, plus up to 2 tons lashed to a roof with no weapons on it. Power: nuclear standard; Solid Oxide and electric versions are offered. Weapons: up to four hovercycle weapon options on the truck, one per side and two on the roof; about half of buyers fit none, and most who do arm the APC with 2-4. Only Solid Oxide and nuclear trucks can power a wired-in weapon. Modules: Medical, an ambulance and field clinic with four roll-out gurneys, six treatment stations, ten of each medical nanobot set, two portable Bio-Scan & Bio-Labs, a Portable Laboratory, and red lights and a siren. Police Mobile Command, with five computer stations, a Criminal I.D. Recognition System, a dozen big screens, six short-range communicators, radio reaching 500 miles (800 km), radar, cameras and listening gear, a weapons locker for six, two Police Robot Floater Cameras, two robot drone assistants (bought separately) and a Portable Scan Dihilator. Police SWAT, with benches for six in power armor or ten in EBA, a command station showing each officer''s camera live, and a locker of two Urban Warrior EBA, two Huntsman partial armors, neural maces, silver-plated blades, Vibro-Knives, paired specialty weapons (shotgun, rail gun, grenade launcher, flamethrower and the like) with reloads, anti-monster ammunition, 96 grenades of six kinds, 1-4 other drones plus four Robot Floater Cameras, first aid and nanobot kits, restraints, two Bio-Scan & Bio-Labs and a Scan Dihilator, plus sirens, lights and the ram prow; body armor, power armor and extra drones are not included. In many ''Burbs, towns and small kingdoms it outclasses the local lawmen''s whole station, and may be the town''s main communications hub or only vehicle. Police Arrest (''Mobile Lock-Up''), a jail on wheels: two small cells of 1-4 each (8 standing, cramped), one long cell of 6-8 (16 standing), benches by the rear doors for up to four guards, and a cab sealed off from the cells. APC, with a roof laser cannon and a mini-missile turret (see the weapons), the ram prow, and troop space beside the laser''s generator and the spare missiles.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.211-212'),
  ('ng-cw81-ermine', 'NG-CW81 Ermine Wilderness Reconnaissance Vehicle', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'Up to two.', '75 mph (120 km) at most on roads and flat ground; half or less on grassland, sandy desert and rough ground. Woodland and thick brush are likely impassable without a well-worn path, and even then under 10 mph (16 km).', NULL, 'Drives submerged along a lake floor at 18 mph (29 km), to a depth of only 100 ft (30.5 m).', 'Height 4.5 ft (1.4 m); width 6 ft (1.8 m); length 13 ft (4 m)', '1,200 lbs (540 kg)', 125, 1, NULL, 386000, '386,000 credits electric battery or liquid fuel, 645,000 Solid Oxide, 925,000 nuclear (10 year life). Rubber camouflage webbing over the whole vehicle is 1,000 credits more; a weapon is extra.', 'Model NG-CW81 (Class: All-Terrain Reconnaissance Vehicle). From designers Jason Lee, Jeremy Helton and Brian Hatherly for border patrols, Bounty Hunters, Wilderness Scouts and adventurers: a small, armoured go-anywhere machine that doubles as pack mule, camp, lookout, scout car and transport, giving a driver and two riders Mega-Damage protection. The driver can change tire pressure, drive each wheel on its own and raise or lower the body on hydraulics; it is fully environmental like a robot vehicle; it copes with snow and mud and can run fully underwater; and a winch and cable at each end can, worked together, haul it across a river or chasm on a line. Penalties: none on pavement, short grass, dirt roads and stony desert; -10% piloting on gravel and tall grass, -15% on broken roads, sand, and mud or snow to 8 inches (20 cm); deeper mud and snow, forest and dense growth are likely impassable. It crosses dunes and drives under water. Range: 400 miles (640 km) on gasoline, 2,000 miles (3,200 km) per multi-battery electric charge, unlimited on Solid Oxide or nuclear. Cargo: many small compartments, a 4x2x3 ft (1.2 x 0.6 x 0.9 m) trunk, outside webbing, a roof rain catcher feeding a 20 gallon (75 liter) tank, and room on the roof and rear rail for packs and bags. Sensors: the environmental and communications features of a robot vehicle. Lockdown Security System: left in sentry mode it stays sealed and will not start, keys or not, until a code goes into a hidden keypad; a proximity sensor signals a small handheld display when anything human-sized or bigger comes near, and alarm mode flashes the lights and sounds the horn, which scares off dumb animals and monsters but also draws attention; it runs as long as the battery has power. Weapons: none standard; one hovercycle weapon option can be fitted, usually on the roof, and only Solid Oxide and nuclear models can power it from the vehicle.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.212-213'),
  ('ng-ev-011-pathmaker', 'NG-EV-011 Pathmaker Armored Exploration Vehicle', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'Two: a pilot and a co-pilot or communications engineer.', 'Eight in comfort (12 cramped); another dozen, or three dozen standing, can fit in the cargo area, other rooms and outside for short trips, and 12-16 can ride on top below 40 mph (64 km).', '75 mph (120 km) maximum on pavement and most ground; 45 mph (72 km) on sandy desert, broken pavement, debris-strewn paths and very hard terrain. Only woodland and narrow mountain trails are likely impassable without a wide, well-worn path, and even then under 20 mph (32 km).', NULL, 'Fully submersible: crosses a lake or sea bed at 30 mph (48 km/26 knots) to 1,000 ft (305 m). Best kept to short trips through shallow lakes, swamps and rivers; big, deep, rough water may sink or wreck it.', 'Height 19 ft 7 in (6 m); width 20 ft (6.1 m); length 26 ft 8 in (8.1 m)', '28.7 tons fully loaded', 390, 1, NULL, 16000000, '16 million credits new and fully loaded; 9-11 million used or rebuilt, likely 10% short on Turret Housing and Main Body M.D.C.; as little as 6 million rebuilt with no weapons and 20% less M.D.C. Good availability from Northern Gun, fair from its other sellers. Optional weapons are never discounted.', 'Model type EPC, Exploration Personnel Carrier (Class: Scientific Transport Vehicle and Military Personnel Carrier). Released in 105 P.A. for wilderness travel and exploration: four tread wheels give it grip and balance on snow, ice, mud, sand and uneven ground, and it is quietly amphibious. Inside are a dining and lounge area seating ten, a small lab for 2-3, a tiny kitchen and pantry, one private bedroom, two small washrooms, a bunk room of ten bunks with floor space for two more, and an 8x12x9 ft (2.4 x 3.7 x 2.7 m) cargo bay. The outside is covered in handholds, hooks and brackets for strapping on tools and supplies, and the top can take two more light or medium vehicle weapons. It tows a trailer and load of up to 36 tons. Its upper housing turns 360 degrees like a tank turret and carries two banks of three spotlights, the main gun, and a heavy winch and crane for towing trailers, freeing stuck vehicles or robots, and clearing wreckage, trees and boulders. Entry is by two heavy airlock hatches between the treads or two small hatches on the turret, where the pilot and co-pilot sit; the living spaces are below. Not a tank, it serves armies as an APC, supply carrier and engineering support vehicle, and explorers as a fortified mobile home. With the Main Body gone it cannot move, but the turret, spotlights, main gun and crane still work and turn; destroying the Upper Turret Housing knocks out sensors, optics and communications and shuts it down. The turret, treads and other large external areas need a Called Shot (no penalty), or damage goes to the Main Body. Penalties: none on most ground; -10% piloting on sandy desert and ice and through thick brush, mud or snow; -20% over mountains, boulder fields, cratered ground and debris. It handles snow to 8 ft (2.4 m), mud to 5 ft (1.5 m) and packed snow with ease. Range: effectively unlimited; nuclear only, about 10 years. Searchlights: the six (item 2 of its weapon list) can light one target together or six apart, or flood a wide area. Sensors: as a robot vehicle''s, in a fully enclosed environmental hull.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.214-215');

